# .license-allowlist — accepted-risk register for the dependency licence gate.
#
# Enforced by `scripts/check-dependency-licenses.mjs`, which runs over the
# PRODUCTION dependency tree only (devDependencies are never distributed, so
# their licences cannot affect how the image is conveyed).
#
# The gate denies by default. A package listed here is an ACCEPTED RISK, not a
# cleared one — same posture as `.trivyignore`. Every line must carry a reason
# after `#`; the check refuses a bare package name, because an exception nobody
# justified is indistinguishable from one nobody noticed.
#
# Reviewed 2026-08-07.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# @nangohq/node, @nangohq/types — Elastic License 2.0
#
# THE ONE TO WATCH. The published packages contain no LICENSE file at all (only
# `dist/` and `package.json`), and `package.json` says only "SEE LICENSE IN
# LICENSE FILE IN GIT REPOSITORY". That repository — NangoHQ/nango — is licensed
# Elastic License 2.0. GitHub classifies it as NOASSERTION / "Other", which is
# why no scanner has ever flagged it.
#
# ELv2 imposes usage restrictions (no managed-service resale, no circumventing
# licence-key functionality, no removing notices). AGPL-3.0 §7 forbids imposing
# further restrictions on recipients. So conveying the combined work under
# AGPL-3.0 while part of it is ELv2 is a conflict.
#
# Precisely scoped: the public REPO is fine — it ships our source plus a
# dependency reference, not the library. The exposure is the distributed IMAGE,
# which contains both. `@nangohq/node` is a direct production dependency
# (package.json) imported by src/lib/integrations/nango.ts, so it is traced into
# .next/standalone and does ship.
#
# Held open pending legal review of whether an in-process npm dependency forms a
# "combined work" here. Until that lands, the operative mitigation is: no PUBLIC
# artifact may contain it. Remove this entry if the dependency is dropped or
# isolated behind an optional interface.
@nangohq/node   # ELv2 inside an AGPL distribution — see block above; pending legal review, mitigate by keeping affected artifacts non-public
@nangohq/types  # ELv2, same origin and same review as @nangohq/node; types-only but conveyed identically
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# buffers@0.1.1 — no licence declared anywhere
#
# Transitive: exceljs -> unzipper -> binary -> buffers. Declares no `license`
# field and ships no LICENCE file, so strictly it grants no rights at all.
# Ancient (2011), tiny, and ubiquitous; the practical risk is low, but a
# customer's procurement scan will flag it, so it is recorded here rather than
# discovered by them. Resolved by exceljs dropping unzipper, not by us.
buffers  # no licence declared; transitive via exceljs->unzipper->binary, low practical risk, flagged for procurement awareness
